<% content_for :title, "Terms of Service" %>

<main class="policy">
  <div class="policy__wrap">
    <header class="policy__head">
      <p class="policy__kicker">Salato</p>
      <h1 class="policy__title">Terms of Service</h1>
      <p class="policy__meta">Last updated <%= Date.current.strftime("%-d %B %Y") %></p>
    </header>

    <section class="policy__intro">
      <p>
        These terms govern your use of Salato, an online platform that event organisers
        use to sell tickets and that attendees use to buy them. By creating an account,
        publishing an event, or purchasing a ticket, you agree to these terms. If you're
        agreeing on behalf of an organisation, you confirm you're authorised to do so.
        Please read them alongside our
        <a href="/acceptable-use">Acceptable Use Policy</a>,
        <a href="/refunds">Refund &amp; Cancellation Policy</a>, and
        <a href="/delivery">Ticket Delivery Policy</a>, which form part of this agreement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="who-we-are">
      <h2>Who we are and what Salato does</h2>
      <p>
        Salato provides the software that lets organisers create events, sell tickets,
        and admit attendees. Salato is not the organiser of any event and is not a party
        to the contract between an organiser and a buyer. The event — its content,
        timing, venue, and delivery — is the responsibility of the organiser who lists
        it. Salato is not a bank, is not a payment institution, and does not hold or
        control user funds.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="accounts">
      <h2>Accounts</h2>
      <p>
        To sell tickets you need an organiser account, created through our signup, which
        also sets up your organisation record. You're responsible for the accuracy of the
        information you provide, for keeping your login details secure, and for everything
        done under your account, including by your staff and scanners. Tell us promptly at
        <a href="mailto:hello@salato.app">hello@salato.app</a> if you believe your account
        has been compromised. You must be able to form a legally binding contract to use
        Salato.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="verification">
      <h2>Organiser verification</h2>
      <p>
        Before an organiser can publish a paid event or receive settlement, Salato
        verifies the organiser's identity and, where applicable, business registration
        and tax details, together with a settlement account held in the verified name.
        Each verified organiser is connected to their own account with our payment
        partner. Salato may decline, suspend, or reverse verification, and may request
        further information at any time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="payments">
      <h2>Payments, commission, and settlement</h2>
      <p>
        Ticket payments are processed by our third-party payment partner. At the point of
        each transaction, the payment is split: the organiser's share settles directly to
        the organiser's own connected account, and Salato receives only its platform
        commission — 6% by default, and as otherwise agreed with the organiser. Salato
        does not take receipt of the organiser's funds, does not hold funds before
        disbursement, and does not operate wallets, stored balances, or transfers of value
        between users. Settlement timing to organisers follows the payment partner's
        standard cycle. Your use of the payment partner is also subject to that partner's
        own terms.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="tickets">
      <h2>Tickets and entry</h2>
      <p>
        On payment, Salato issues a digital ticket — a PDF with the attendee's name, a
        unique ticket number, and a single-use QR code — delivered by email and WhatsApp
        as set out in the <a href="/delivery">Ticket Delivery Policy</a>. Tickets are for
        the named event only. Tickets must not be forged, duplicated, resold for profit
        where the organiser prohibits it, or otherwise misused. A QR code that has already
        been scanned is treated as used. The organiser controls admission at the event.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="organiser-obligations">
      <h2>Organiser obligations</h2>
      <p>As an organiser, you agree to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>List only genuine events you're authorised to run, and describe them accurately.</li>
        <li>Set clear, fair refund terms and honour them, and refund in full when you cancel an event.</li>
        <li>Deliver the event as advertised, and comply with all licences, permits, and laws that apply to it.</li>
        <li>Handle attendee data lawfully and only for running your event, in line with the Acceptable Use Policy and applicable data protection law.</li>
        <li>Keep enough funds available to cover refunds you may owe.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="buyer-obligations">
      <h2>Buyer obligations</h2>
      <p>
        As a buyer, you agree to provide accurate contact details, to buy tickets for
        genuine use, not to make fraudulent payments or misuse tickets, and to deal with
        refund requests through the process in the
        <a href="/refunds">Refund &amp; Cancellation Policy</a>. Your contract for the
        event itself is with the organiser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="refunds-disputes">
      <h2>Refunds and disputes</h2>
      <p>
        Refunds, cancellations, and postponements are governed by our
        <a href="/refunds">Refund &amp; Cancellation Policy</a>. Salato may mediate
        disputes between buyers and organisers, and where an event has clearly not been
        delivered as advertised, Salato may suspend the organiser and support affected
        buyers with refunds through the payment partner. Chargebacks follow the payment
        partner's dispute process.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="acceptable-use">
      <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
      <p>
        Your use of Salato must follow our <a href="/acceptable-use">Acceptable Use
        Policy</a>. Among other things, Salato may not be used to sell physical goods,
        to sell tickets to unlawful or harmful events, to move money for any purpose
        other than paying for tickets, or to compromise the platform's security. Breaches
        may lead to removal of content, suspension, held or reversed payouts, or account
        closure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="ip">
      <h2>Intellectual property</h2>
      <p>
        Salato, its name, logo, and software are owned by us and protected by law. We
        grant you a limited, non-exclusive, non-transferable right to use the platform
        for its intended purpose. Content you upload — event details, images, and copy —
        remains yours, but you grant Salato the licence needed to host and display it so
        we can provide the service. You're responsible for having the rights to any
        content you upload.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="availability">
      <h2>Service availability</h2>
      <p>
        We work to keep Salato available and reliable, but we don't guarantee it will be
        uninterrupted or error-free. We may modify, suspend, or discontinue features, and
        may carry out maintenance that affects availability. We'll aim to give notice of
        significant planned changes where we can.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="liability">
      <h2>Liability</h2>
      <p>
        Because Salato is not the organiser, we're not liable for the events themselves —
        whether they take place, their quality, or anything that happens at them; those
        are the organiser's responsibility. To the fullest extent permitted by law, Salato
        is not liable for indirect or consequential loss, and our total liability to you
        for any claim relating to the platform is limited to the fees Salato earned on the
        transaction giving rise to the claim. Nothing in these terms excludes liability
        that cannot be excluded by law.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="termination">
      <h2>Suspension and termination</h2>
      <p>
        You may stop using Salato at any time. We may suspend or close an account that
        breaches these terms or our policies, that poses a risk to attendees, buyers, or
        the platform, or where required by law. Where a breach is serious — including
        fraud or anything that endangers people — we may act immediately and without
        notice, and may report the matter to the relevant authorities.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="changes">
      <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
      <p>
        We may update these terms from time to time. The current version always lives at
        this page, with the date it was last updated shown at the top. If we make a
        significant change, we'll aim to let account holders know. Continuing to use
        Salato after a change means you accept the updated terms.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="law">
      <h2>Governing law</h2>
      <p>
        These terms are governed by the laws of Kenya, and the courts of Kenya have
        jurisdiction over any dispute relating to them, without affecting any consumer
        rights you may have under the law of your own country.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy__section" id="contact">
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Salato — <a href="mailto:hello@salato.app">hello@salato.app</a>. For anything
        about these terms, your account, an event, or a ticket, that's the address to use.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="policy__foot">
      <p>
        By using Salato you agree to these terms and the policies referenced within them.
      </p>
    </footer>
  </div>
</main>

<style>
  .policy {
    --ink: #150D3A;
    --violet: #7620B1;
    --orange: #FC8027;
    --mist: #F7F4FB;
    background: var(--mist);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: "Inter Tight", "Trebuchet MS", system-ui, sans-serif;
    padding: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem) 1.25rem clamp(3rem, 7vw, 6rem);
  }
  .policy__wrap { max-width: 46rem; margin: 0 auto; }
  .policy__head { padding-bottom: 2rem; border-bottom: 2px solid rgba(21,13,58,.1); margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .policy__kicker {
    font-family: "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: .8rem; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--violet);
    margin: 0 0 .75rem;
  }
  .policy__title {
    font-family: "Outfit", "Trebuchet MS", sans-serif;
    font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.05;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.2rem);
    margin: 0 0 .9rem;
    background: linear-gradient(100deg, var(--violet), var(--orange));
    -webkit-background-clip: text; background-clip: text; color: transparent;
  }
  .policy__meta { margin: 0; color: rgba(21,13,58,.6); font-size: .95rem; }
  .policy__intro p, .policy__section p, .policy__foot p { line-height: 1.65; }
  .policy__intro p { font-size: 1.08rem; margin: 0 0 2.5rem; }
  .policy__section { margin-bottom: 2.75rem; }
  .policy__section h2 {
    font-family: "Outfit", "Trebuchet MS", sans-serif;
    font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 .8rem;
    padding-left: .85rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--orange);
  }
  .policy__section p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .policy__section ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; }
  .policy__section li { line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: .55rem; }
  .policy__section strong { font-weight: 600; }
  .policy a { color: var(--violet); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
  .policy a:hover { color: var(--orange); }
  .policy__foot {
    margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1.75rem;
    border-top: 2px solid rgba(21,13,58,.1);
    color: rgba(21,13,58,.7); font-size: .95rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .policy__section h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; }
  }
</style>
